SupraLINUX KPipeWire integration

Integrate unchanged official stable sources. Preserve the three SONAME 6 shared
libraries, Ubuntu binary package names, installed public SDK/CMake targets, all
three QML modules (source, record and monitor), translations and symbol guards.
Qt, PipeWire, FFmpeg, libva and other external runtimes stay with Ubuntu. The
existing Plasma Wayland Protocols 1.20 definition supplies stream_virtual_output;
this package does not need the separate newer KWayland protocol transition.
Enable and run both TestEncoder and mediamonitortest, including every row and
upstream's explicit unavailable-codec/hardware skips. Record full verbose CTest
output. The upstream process wrapper uses disposable HOME/XDG state, an isolated
session bus, offscreen software rendering and the upstream KDECI null sink.
Do not modify upstream tests or start host desktop multimedia services.

The reviewed Ubuntu client uses only installed SDK headers and CMake targets.
It connects the actual installed library to its own private PipeWire daemon and
one synthetic camera source with explicit zero-offset MemFd buffers and exact
160x120 RGBA pixels. Verify repeated frames, independent frame copy/recycling,
all installed QML modules, MediaMonitor source detection/filter/reconnect and
inherited role enums. Record actual VP8 software output, at least ten decoded
frames and a full FFmpeg decode; preserve the output and original logs. Keep the
same Ubuntu client/producer bytes across the complete six-binary candidate APT
upgrade and preserve Qt/PipeWire/FFmpeg versions, then build and run a consumer.
All private owned processes and state are bounded and removed on exit.

Preliminary Ubuntu pool-buffer diagnostics failed exact pixel checks. The
mapoffset handling and frame-copy sizing code are unchanged between authenticated
Ubuntu 6.6.4 and signed 6.7.5; these are inherited concerns, not an observed
candidate regression. Zero-offset delivery and frame-copy checks pass against
actual Ubuntu libraries. QML inherited MediaMonitor role enums are also valid;
the earlier static suspicion was disproved by actual client execution.
This package fixture does not certify nonzero-offset buffers, guarded memory
reads, GPU DMA-BUF/hardware codecs, real portals/compositors, audio devices or
complete desktop screencasting. Those remain explicit integration QA items.
